es to two top positions in the  private sector in there's now a quota in  Germany for supervisory boards of large  companies so that they are slowly  getting to the 30% quota that is  required of women on supervisory boards  but it's had very little effect so far  on operational management their women  are still less than 10% of the actual  operational management so lots of room  for improvement there as well and other  theories as to why Germany is so far  behind given its it's generally  progressive reputation well for a long  time and given the fact that the most  powerful woman in the world is the head  of the German government  a supreme irony if you will but there  the theories going back to the period of  the Cold War were that West Germany was  very eager to maintain conservative  social traditions putting women  essentially in the kitchen and in the  nursery part partly in contrast to  eastern Germany where women's rights  were very very strong but of course that  was 30 years ago that the Berlin Wall  fell so there has been changed since  then  but it has been slow for example  throughout many parts of Germany it's  hard for families to find a kindergarten  place even to this day many schools are  going until lunchtime and then release  the children sending them home with the  expectation that there will be a mother  at home to provide a warm lunch all of  that is changing but it's changing very  slowly this government has made some of  those issues priorities but as I say  there's still a lot of work to be done  well in decried and  Oberlin for now thank you
 

